course: everyday
lesson: 59
title: Staying safe and kind online
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Some information is private and stays off the internet: your full name, home address, school, phone number, birthday, passwords and photos that show where you live; share passwords only with your parents.
explain: People online may not be who they say: a «10-year-old» in a game chat could be an adult; never agree to meet someone you know only online, and tell a parent if anyone asks for photos, secrets or a meeting.
explain: Words online hurt just as much as words said aloud, and a post or photo can be copied and kept forever, so before you send something, ask: «Would I say this to their face? Would I be happy for my grandma to see it?»
explain: If you see something upsetting or someone is mean to you, do not reply; tell a trusted adult, save a screenshot, then block and report; you will not be in trouble for telling.
explain: Screens are fun, but children also need active play, time with people and 9 to 12 hours of sleep; a boy in Colorado who switched off his tablet an hour before bed found he fell asleep faster, because the bright screen and exciting games keep the brain awake.
explain: A girl in Canada chatted online with someone who said he was 12 and liked her favourite band; he asked to meet her alone, and when her parents checked, he was a grown man, and the police stepped in.
explain: A boy in New Zealand sent a silly photo to one friend; it was shared all over school within a day, and he could not get it back.
fact: Your full name, address, school, phone number and passwords are private information.
fact: You share your passwords only with your parents or carers.
fact: People online can pretend to be someone they are not.
fact: You should never meet someone you know only from the internet.
fact: Things posted online can be copied and kept, even after you delete them.
fact: Mean words online hurt just as much as mean words said in person.
fact: If something online upsets you, you tell a trusted adult.
fact: You do not reply to mean messages; you save them, block the sender and report them.
fact: Clicking on unknown links or pop-ups can bring viruses or tricks.
fact: Most social media apps say users must be at least 13 years old, because younger children need extra protection from what they might meet there; some countries set a higher age.
fact: When children say two people are «going out» or «together», they mean the two are boyfriend and girlfriend, or a couple; that is their private business.
fact: Divorce means a mum and dad stop being married; it is hard for their children, so jokes about it hurt.
fact: Children aged 6 to 12 need about 9 to 12 hours of sleep a night.
fact: Using screens just before bed can make it harder to fall asleep.
fact: People online can lie about their age, name and who they are.
fact: Once a photo or message is shared online, you cannot fully take it back.
exercise: Which of these is private information you should not share online? | Your home address | Your favourite colour | The name of your favourite book | Your favourite animal
exercise: Who should know your passwords? | Only your parents or carers | Your best friend at school | Anyone you play games with online | Nobody at all, not even your parents
exercise: A player in a game chat says he is 10 and asks for your phone number. What do you do? | Do not give it, and tell a parent | Give it, because he is your age | Give it only if he gives his first | Give your address instead of the number
exercise: Why can you not be sure who someone online really is? | People can use fake names, ages and pictures | Everyone online must show their real face on camera | Websites check that everyone is honest | Children online are always children
exercise: Your online friend wants to meet you at the park and says not to tell your parents. What do you do? | Do not go, and tell your parents at once | Go, but only in the daytime | Go, but bring a friend along | Go, because you have chatted for months and you know him well
exercise: Why should you think before posting a photo? | It can be copied and kept forever, even if deleted | Photos disappear after one day | Only your best friends can ever see it, wherever you post it | Deleting a photo removes every copy
exercise: Someone sends you a mean message. What should you NOT do? | Send a mean message back | Tell a trusted adult | Take a screenshot to save it | Block and report the sender
exercise: A pop-up says «You won a free phone! Click here!». What is wise? | Do not click, close it and tell an adult | Click quickly before the prize goes to someone else | Type in your address to get the phone | Share it with friends so they win too
exercise: Why do mean words online hurt? | A real person reads them and feels them | They do not hurt, because they are only text | They only hurt if they have spelling mistakes | They only hurt if they are spoken aloud
exercise: About how much sleep do children aged 6 to 12 need each night? | About 9 to 12 hours | About 5 to 6 hours | About 7 hours at most | About 14 to 16 hours
exercise: Why is it a good idea to stop using screens before bedtime? | Bright screens and exciting games keep the brain awake | Screens use up far too much electricity late at night | Screens get too hot after dark | Screens only work during the day
exercise: You see a video online that scares you. What should you do? | Close it and tell a trusted adult | Watch it again to stop being scared | Keep it secret so you do not lose your tablet | Send it to friends to see if they are scared
exercise: Before you send a message, what is a good question to ask yourself? | Would I say this to the person's face? | Will this get me lots of likes? | Is this message long enough? | Will anyone know that it was me?
exercise: Why is it unsafe to post a photo of yourself in your school uniform with your street behind you? | It shows strangers where you go to school and live | Uniform photos are against the law | The photo will look old-fashioned when you grow up | It might use too much storage space
test: A girl in Sydney wants to post «Home alone till 6, come round!» on a public page. What is the danger? | Strangers could learn she is alone at home | Her friends might all come round much too early | The post would be too short to read | Nobody would be interested in it
test: Which username is safest for a 10-year-old? | StarDragon42 | Emily_Jones_Houston | MiaSmith2015_Elm_Street | Jake_Room4_Lincoln_School
test: An online game friend asks for a photo and says «It's our secret». What does this tell you? | It is a warning sign, so stop and tell a parent | He is a good friend who likes you | Sending one photo is fine if it is a nice one | It is fine as long as you do not show your face
test: A boy in Manchester keeps getting nasty messages from a classmate. What is the best plan? | Do not reply, save them, block and tell a trusted adult | Reply with worse messages so it stops | Delete everything so nobody ever finds out | Close his account and say nothing to anyone
test: What would probably happen if you played games on a tablet in bed until late every night? | You would feel tired and find school harder | You would fall asleep faster each night | Your eyes would get much stronger | Your brain would need less sleep over time
test: Why is it wrong to share a funny but embarrassing photo of a friend without asking? | It could hurt them and cannot be taken back | Photos of friends are always against the rules | It is fine if the photo is really funny | It is only wrong if your friend is crying in it
test: Which is true about deleting a post? | Others may already have copied or saved it | Deleting removes it from everywhere forever | Nobody can see a post after one hour | Posts can only be seen by your friends
test: An email says your game account will close unless you send your password. What is it most likely to be? | A trick to steal your account | A real message from the game company | A message from your teacher | A friendly reminder from your parents
test: Why do many apps say users must be at least 13? | Younger children need extra protection from what they might meet | Children under 13 cannot read well enough | Phones do not work for younger children | It is only to sell more phones to older children
test: game friend wants my address to send me a skin lol | Do not give it, your address is private | Give it, he is a friend from the game | Give it if he sends his first | Give just the street, not the number
test: online mate is 11 like me he says, wants to meet at the park sat | Never meet someone you only know online | Go if he is really 11 years old | Go but bring a friend your age | Go if the park is near your house and lots of people are there
test: someone sent me mean msgs on the group chat 😢 reply? | Do not reply; save, block, report and tell an adult | Reply with something meaner back | Delete them all and say nothing | Leave the chat and forget it, it'll stop if you ignore it
test: i'll post it then delete it in 5 min, no one'll see | Things online can be copied and kept, even after deleting | Deleted posts are gone forever at once | Nobody can copy a post in 5 minutes, it's too quick to screenshot | Only posts kept for a day are saved
test: met someone online, says he's 11, wants to meet at the park, just us | Tell a parent, never meet alone, he may not be a child | Go, he said he is 11 | Go if you bring a friend | Go if it is in the day and he sends a selfie first
test: someone online asked for a pic of me and said keep it secret | Do not send it and tell a trusted adult | Send one with your face only | Send it and delete it after | Send it if they are nice and promise not to show it to anyone
test: Lucas says u can always delete a photo once it's out there. right? | No, once shared it can be copied anywhere | Yes, deleting removes it everywhere | Yes, if you are quick | Yes, unless it went viral
test: heard a kid met someone off a game who said he was 11 but turned out to be a grown man, they reckon the police came | Either way, people online can pretend, so never meet online-only friends | It proves games are only for adults | It means you can meet people if they send a photo of themselves first | It is gossip, so online friends are always safe
test: my mate says if u delete a post it's gone forever so u can post anything | Wrong; posts can be copied and kept even after you delete them | He is right, delete means gone | He is right, if you delete it quickly | He is right, only adults know how to save posts, so kids can post whatever they like
test: someone made a group chat to laugh at Chloe's photo, they added me, should i join in | No; leave it, save it and tell a trusted adult, because mean words online hurt | Yes, just to see what they say | Yes, as long as you do not post | Yes, but tell Chloe it was a joke and that you only joined in so they would not pick on you
exam: Which of the following constitutes private information that ought not to be shared online? | One's full name, address, school, telephone number and passwords | One's favourite animal | One's favourite colour | The name of a favourite book
exam: Identify the persons with whom a child may share his passwords. | His parents or carers only | His closest friends | Anyone who asks politely | His classmates and teachers
exam: It being given that a mean message is received, select the course of action consistent with established guidance. | Not to reply, but to save it, block the sender and report it | To reply with an equally mean message | To delete it and tell nobody | To forward it to all one's friends so that they may advise what is to be done
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance concerning unknown links and pop-ups. | Clicking upon them may bring viruses or deceptions | Clicking upon them is always safe | They are always from trusted companies | They improve the speed of the device
test: someone in the group chat posted "Mia n Leo r going out ❤️" and everyone sending laughing emojis | They are boyfriend and girlfriend; that is private, so do not join in or share it | It means they are going outside to play, so send more emojis | It means they had a fight, so screenshot it for everyone | It means they are leaving school, so tell the teacher
test: big kid sent me a pic of a teacher n said "they're together, pass it on" | He claims the teachers are a couple; do not forward it, and tell a trusted adult | Forward it, everyone should know | Add a funny caption and post it | Reply with a worse rumour about another teacher, so the chat moves on from this one
test: my mate's mum n dad divorcing n kids are making memes bout it | Divorce means parents stop being married; the memes hurt your mate, so tell an adult | Divorce means a party, so the memes are fine | Share the memes but say it was not you | Ask your mate to make one too, then it's not mean because he's joining in with everyone
